---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import OhmsLaw from '@/components/tools/calc2/OhmsLaw.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="ohms-law-calculator">
  <OhmsLaw client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the Ohm's law calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      Ohm's law links the voltage across a resistor, the current through it and its resistance. With
      the power law it ties four values together, so any two of them give the other two. Pick the two
      you know, enter them with their units, and the Ohm's law calculator works out the rest.
    </p>
    <pre><code>V = I × R        I = V ÷ R        R = V ÷ I
P = V × I        P = I² × R       P = V² ÷ R
V = √(P × R)     I = √(P ÷ R)</code></pre>
    <p>
      V is in volts, I in amperes, R in ohms and P in watts. The fields take prefixes: 1 kΩ is 1,000 Ω,
      1 mA is 0.001 A.
    </p>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A 12 V battery is connected across a 6 Ω resistor. The current is I = 12 ÷ 6 = 2 A, and the power
      the resistor turns into heat is P = 12² ÷ 6 = 24 W. A resistor rated at 1/4 W would burn out; pick
      one rated at 50 W or more for a margin.
    </p>
    <p>
      For an LED on a 5 V supply with a 2 V forward voltage at 20 mA, the resistor drops 3 V, so
      R = 3 ÷ 0.02 = 150 Ω and P = 3 × 0.02 = 0.06 W.
    </p>

    <h2>The animation</h2>
    <p>
      The dots show charge moving the way conventional current flows: out of the + terminal, through the
      resistor and back to the − terminal. They move faster when the current is larger. The speed is
      not to scale: real electrons drift at fractions of a millimetre per second, while the signal
      travels close to the speed of light.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Ohm's law holds for resistors at a constant temperature. A lamp filament or a heater changes its resistance as it warms up.</li>
      <li>Diodes, LEDs and transistors are not ohmic: their current does not grow in proportion to the voltage.</li>
      <li>For alternating current with capacitors or inductors, use impedance in place of resistance.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
